#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d62f753-a3e0-5c7e-919d-3f878c1d8e93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Measure of Unknown Angles on a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f80b711-19ed-52be-8297-190fa8f36a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180-10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f80b711-19ed-52be-8297-190fa8f36a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180-10x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510929} :answer-field/id #uuid "e17cb2f5-7c1b-57d6-8e9f-2a843b07d5bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99fbfa91-9f2d-531e-9a33-54900502faa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-142504" :question/problem "![](images/f3/f3f38ca1b55a12dcaffc5a52eb4f08fc6d6ec3d5c746cb8215ad3e0ed7671153.png)\n\nFind an expression in terms of $x$ for the measure of $∠COD.$\n\n$m∠COD=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $∠BOE$ is a straight angle, so\n\n$$\nm∠BOE={180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, the angle addition postulate gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOC+m∠COD+m∠DOE & =m∠BOE \\\\ (5x+5)^{∘}+m∠COD+(5x-5)^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 10{x}^{∘}+m∠COD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠COD & =(180-10x)^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105694 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fa963eb-82a7-56cb-a8b8-f9291adec2d0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Length of a Line Segment With Endpoints on a Circle and Passing Through the Center"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35f29ca2-0fa4-5b57-89ea-772c55ec32c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35f29ca2-0fa4-5b57-89ea-772c55ec32c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510903} :answer-field/id #uuid "09f71703-4d67-5e26-a308-758da21e5e6f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c19df22d-0aea-5f35-9eda-6b27f1f826b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-142509" :question/problem "If $AD=8,$ then $OC=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the points $A,C,$ and $D$ lie on the circle. We know that the radius of the circle is $r=OC.$\n\nWe first notice that\n\n$$\nAD=AO+OD.\n$$\n\nNow, $\\overset{―}{AO}$ and $\\overset{―}{OD}$ are both segments between the center of the circle and the circle itself. So, they are both radii of the circle, and because $AD=8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =8 \\\\ AO+OD & =8 \\\\ r+r & =8 \\\\ 2r & =8 \\\\ r & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nOC=r=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105738 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02d46a44-0f02-5491-8e71-2eb22ad50ff9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Measure of an Angle Using the Isosceles Triangle Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "964ae523-a001-56a9-918a-6ed65ac952c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "964ae523-a001-56a9-918a-6ed65ac952c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511094} :answer-field/id #uuid "d30ec5d2-7185-5122-ad13-5d37e66f9e20" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "839696c7-ab7c-5fbc-a053-f39b3696e2a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-142517" :question/problem "![](images/72/72c697336f9518e5be1c8b89d9244c0952a7b8f5e77f007638e6a9405d98d8fd.png)\n\nFind an expression in terms of $x$ for the measure of $∠ODC.$\n\n$m∠ODC=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\overset{―}{OC}$ and $\\overset{―}{OD}$ are radii of the circle, the triangle $△OCD$ is isosceles. Consequently, the base angles must have the same measure:\n\n$$\n∠OCD=∠ODC.\n$$\n\nAlso, from step 3, we know that $m∠COD={(180-10x)}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/50/509c6fd7ffabd5c4f7b71e89dd94cb29c374dd6d2d64407d65bcdef44553c60e.png)\n\nNow, since the internal angles of any triangle sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠ODC+m∠OCD+m∠COD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠ODC+(m∠ODC)+(180-10x)^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2m∠ODC-10{x}^{∘} & ={0}^{∘} \\\\ 2m∠ODC & =10{x}^{∘} \\\\ m∠ODC & =5{x}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $m∠ODC=5{x}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4b0977a-6bcb-5d7f-bec8-b10bff6d7e23" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Alternate Interior Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "527947cd-9a5e-5103-893d-eef7754c5992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "527947cd-9a5e-5103-893d-eef7754c5992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510960} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d4044b8-9993-5869-ad0e-3ffc963c1a2e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511345} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1bd62cd-fffe-5e11-866e-8b077124e4eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1bd62cd-fffe-5e11-866e-8b077124e4eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511280} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca229fee-40f1-5f93-90d7-5d892f402841" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c18e889b-e4ac-54b0-ba7c-c7c1982b19f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-142519" :question/problem "![](images/82/828ac748b9d8c87e4a7a66dfddb2e926a3362830d551653260b5974b6bd2ca1a.png)\n\nGiven that the line segments $\\overset{―}{CD}$ and $\\overset{―}{AF}$ are parallel and $m∠FAO={(3x+18)}^{∘},$ find the measures of $∠FAO$ and $∠AOE.$\n\n$∠FAO=$\n{{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}$\n\n$∠AOE=$\n{{field-2}}\n${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "The parallel line segments $\\overset{―}{CD}∥\\overset{―}{AF}$ are cut by the transversal $\\overset{―}{AD}.$\n\nThe angles $∠ODC$ and $∠FAO$ are interior to the parallel line segments and are on opposite sides of the transversal. So, they are alternate interior angles and consequently, they have the same measure.\n\nIn step 4, we found that $m∠ODC=5{x}^{∘}.$\n\nSetting the measures of $∠ODC$ and $∠FAO$ equal to each other, we have an equation that we can solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠ODC & =m∠FAO \\\\ 5{x}^{∘} & =(3x+18)^{∘} \\\\ 5x & =3x+18 \\\\ 2x & =18 \\\\ x & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=9$ into the expressions for the measures of $∠FAO$ and $∠AOE$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠FAO & =(3x+18)^{∘} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 9+18)^{∘} \\\\  & ={45}^{∘} \\\\ m∠AOE & =(185-5x)^{∘} \\\\  & =(185-5\\cdot 9)^{∘} \\\\  & ={140}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $m∠FAO={45}^{∘}$ and $m∠AOE={140}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110630 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e6e34d9-29a2-55ed-9d28-258cbfd91600" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Quantity Using Supplementary Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd13dfdd-ebf1-5572-a777-5c750dd784e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "185-5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd13dfdd-ebf1-5572-a777-5c750dd784e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "185-5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511170} :answer-field/id #uuid "deda8d33-afb6-5faa-bc94-281563101948" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3360eb5-04da-59fb-81da-119f0aeb047a" :question/math-academy-id "q-142529" :question/problem "![](images/96/96a76584c5ddbcc39d221334803c6791cb3b459178e70c310e4eca54a4e85462.png)\n\nFind an expression in terms of $x$ for the measure of the angle $∠AOE.$\n\n$∠AOE=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $∠AOD$ is a straight angle, the angles $∠AOE$ and $∠DOE$ are supplementary. Two angles are supplementary if their measures add up to ${180}^{∘}.$ \n\nSo, the measure of the angle $∠AOE$ that's supplementary to the angle of $∠DOE=5x-{5}^{∘}$ must be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠AOE & ={180}^{∘}-(5x-5)^{∘} \\\\  & =({180}^{∘}+{5}^{∘})-5{x}^{∘} \\\\  & ={(185-5x)}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105718 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bad93a16-17d4-50ff-89c8-f3d966df5f1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unknown Quantities in Quadrilaterals and Pentagons"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c4e7a13-93c7-5102-ac8c-81d508b2eeb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "110" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c4e7a13-93c7-5102-ac8c-81d508b2eeb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "110" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511109} :answer-field/id #uuid "792a9d88-7c6f-5b31-aae0-b4d4d44254ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db1a27df-5d87-50c3-9b60-42007a434557" :question/math-academy-id "q-142717" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4ba93d3eb8944d086b001c0aa01cb28251705a6af9e55a9fd58e2affce12acaa.png)\n\nIn the quadrilateral $AOEF,$ the angle $∠E$ measures ${65}^{∘}.$ Then, $m∠F=${{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "A quadrilateral has $n=4$ sides, so the sum of its interior angles must be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom step 5, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & =m∠FAO={45}^{∘}, \\\\ m∠O & =m∠AOE={140}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/f6/f67441e3999a8b81e7ac63555c729f0f6c31bff015477efc3031364c6fb22003.png)\n\nAdding up the interior angles, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠O+m∠E+m∠F & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {45}^{∘}+{140}^{∘}+{65}^{∘}+m∠F & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {250}^{∘}+m∠F & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠F & ={110}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $m∠F={110}^{∘}.$"}]}
